{"parentUuid":"ce7696fd-e397-464a-bcd8-f722c4fa17bc","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"43280994-d5ce-4e76-a374-f3f372a3ec93","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A7UsHSUaj8MDzfmC33N3vg","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-05-12-openai-privacy-filter-addon-design.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"37fe772d-9e78-4c2c-8ca2-33f45ec8d4a7","timestamp":"2026-05-13T00:54:20.564Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-05-12-openai-privacy-filter-addon-design.md","content":"# Design: OpenAI Privacy Filter as Optional Redaction Add-on\n\n**Status:** Draft\n**Date:** 2026-05-12\n**Author:** Peyton Montei\n**Worktree branch:** `openai-redact`\n\n## Context\n\nThe `redact` package ([redact/redact.go](../../../redact/redact.go)) runs seven\ndeterministic detection layers that produce a single merged set of\n`taggedRegion`s before substitution:\n\n1. Entropy-based (`secretPattern` + Shannon entropy, threshold 4.5) — always on\n2. Pattern-based (`betterleaks`, ~260 known formats) — always on\n3. Credentialed URIs (`REDACTED`) — always on\n4. Database connection strings (JDBC, keyword DSN, semicolon DSN) — always on\n5. User-defined custom rules (inline + rule packs) — opt-in\n6. Bounded credential key/value pairs (`DB_PASSWORD=REDACTED — always on\n7. Regex-based PII (email, phone, address, custom regex) — opt-in via `ConfigurePII`\n\nEvery layer is regex/entropy and microseconds per call. The detector runs on\nevery checkpoint write — per-turn temporary checkpoints on the shadow branch,\ncondensation to `entire/checkpoints/v1` on commit, and read-side export paths\n(`entire attach`, `entire explain`, `entire doctor bundle`). The\n`RedactedBytes` type enforces at compile time that downstream consumers\n(checkpoint stores, `compact.Compact`) only see redacted bytes.\n\nThe pipeline has a known weak area: contextual PII that regex cannot reason\nabout — most notably **personal names**. The existing regex PII layer covers\nemail/phone/address by shape, not by context, and has no detector for\nperson names at all. Teams that want stronger PII coverage today must either\naccept the gap or write per-name custom rules, which scales poorly.\n\nOpenAI released [openai/privacy-filter](https://github.com/openai/privacy-filter)\non 2026-04-17. It is a 1.5B-parameter (50M active, MoE) bidirectional\ntoken-classification model, Apache 2.0, that labels text into 8 categories:\n\n- `account_number`, `secret` — overlap heavily with betterleaks + entropy\n- `private_email`, `private_phone`, `private_address` — overlap with our regex PII\n- `private_url`, `private_date` — partial / no existing coverage\n- `private_person` — **no existing coverage; the unique capability**\n\nThe model ships as a Python CLI (`opf`) and a Python API. Weights download\nto `~/.opf/privacy_filter` on first run (~1.5 GB). There is no `serve` mode\ntoday; invocations are one-shot. The model card explicitly lists\n\"over-redaction of benign high-entropy strings, placeholders, hashes, sample\ncredentials, or synthetic examples that resemble secrets\" as a documented\nfailure mode — i.e., layering its `secret` category on top of our existing\nsecret detectors would re-introduce exactly the over-redaction problem the\nproject has already tuned away.\n\n### Constraints carried from prior incidents\n\n- **Over-redaction makes logs unusable.** Prior versions of the redaction\n  pipeline were too aggressive; users could not read their own transcripts.\n  Every layer added must avoid regressing this.\n- **Push paths are already long.** Condensation and push add latency to\n  every commit. Adding an unbounded ML call inline to every per-turn write\n  is a non-starter.\n- **Opt-in framing.** This feature is for teams that want stronger PII\n  coverage and are willing to install an extra dependency. Default behavior\n  for users who do nothing must not change.\n\n## Goals\n\n1. Add OpenAI Privacy Filter as an optional eighth detection layer that\n   augments — never replaces — the existing seven.\n2. Run OPF only at the boundaries where redacted bytes leave per-turn local\n   state: condensation to `entire/checkpoints/v1`, and the read-side export\n   paths (`attach`, `explain`, `doctor bundle`). Per-turn temporary writes\n   stay on the fast pipeline.\n3. Default category set is `private_person` only — the genuine new\n   capability — with all seven other categories individually toggleable but\n   off by default.\n4. Fail soft. If OPF is unavailable, crashes, or times out, log an\n   actionable warning and fall back to the full existing seven-layer stack.\n   Never block the commit/push for an opt-in add-on. Teams that want strict\n   guarantees can flip `on_failure: \"block\"`.\n5. Keep the runtime adapter behind an interface so we can swap shell-out\n   for daemon-mode later without churning the detector or any callsite.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- **Replacing any existing layer.** Betterleaks, entropy, custom rules, and\n  the regex PII layer all keep running unchanged.\n- **Auto-installing OPF.** The user runs `pip install` themselves. We do\n  not ship Python, manage a venv, or download weights.\n- **Shipping daemon mode in this iteration.** Documented as a follow-up;\n  the interface boundary is designed to accept it.\n- **Adding new top-level config keys.** OPF settings live as a sibling\n  inside the existing `RedactionSettings`.\n- **Changing default behavior for users who do nothing.** Zero impact if\n  `redaction.openai_privacy_filter` is absent from settings.\n\n## Approach\n\nOPF becomes **layer 8** in `redact.String` — a region producer that emits\n`taggedRegion` entries and rides the existing merge-and-replace machinery\n([redact.go:235-265](../../../redact/redact.go)) for free.\n\nTo keep per-turn writes off the model, the package exposes two entry-point\npairs:\n\n```go\n// Existing — fast, always-on. Called by every per-turn write path.\nfunc String(s string) string\nfunc JSONLBytes(b []byte) (RedactedBytes, error)\n\n// New — fast layers + OPF augmentation. Called only by condensation + export.\nfunc StringWithPrivacyFilter(ctx context.Context, s string) string\nfunc JSONLBytesWithPrivacyFilter(ctx context.Context, b []byte) (RedactedBytes, error)\n```\n\nSplitting at the entry-point level (rather than via a context-scoped flag\non the existing functions) means every callsite that wants OPF says so by\nname. `git grep WithPrivacyFilter` shows the entire augmented surface. The\nplain entry points cannot accidentally activate OPF.\n\n### Asymmetry vs. existing layers (intentional)\n\n| Layer | Per-turn temp writes | Condensation | Export reads |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Layers 1–7 (entropy, betterleaks, URIs, DB strings, custom rules, credential values, regex PII) | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |\n| Layer 8 (OPF, when configured) | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ |\n\nThis is safe for the **push threat model** because only\n`entire/checkpoints/v1` is pushed, and condensation is the gateway to it.\nTemporary shadow-branch checkpoints never leave the machine via push; they\ndo, however, get read out by export commands (`attach`, `explain`,\n`doctor bundle`), so those callsites are also moved to the new entry\npoints.\n\n## Settings schema\n\nA new optional field on `RedactionSettings`\n([settings/settings.go:159](../../../cmd/entire/cli/settings/settings.go)),\nparallel to `PII` and `CustomRedactions`:\n\n```jsonc\n// .entire/settings.json\n{\n  \"redaction\": {\n    \"pii\":               { /* existing, unchanged */ },\n    \"custom_redactions\": { /* existing, unchanged */ },\n\n    \"openai_privacy_filter\": {\n      \"enabled\": false,                       // master switch; default off\n      \"categories\": {                         // per-category opt-in; default all false\n        \"private_person\":   true,             // the recommended default\n        \"private_email\":    false,\n        \"private_phone\":    false,\n        \"private_address\":  false,\n        \"private_url\":      false,\n        \"private_date\":     false,\n        \"secret\":           false,\n        \"account_number\":   false\n      },\n      \"command\":         \"opf\",               // override if not on PATH or pinned to a venv\n      \"timeout_seconds\": 30,                  // per-call hard timeout\n      \"on_failure\":      \"warn\"               // \"warn\" | \"block\"; default \"warn\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nGo struct mirroring the schema:\n\n```go\ntype RedactionSettings struct {\n    PII                 *PIISettings      `json:\"pii,omitempty\"`\n    CustomRedactions    map[string]string `json:\"custom_redactions,omitempty\"`\n    OpenAIPrivacyFilter *OPFSettings      `json:\"openai_privacy_filter,omitempty\"`\n}\n\ntype OPFSettings struct {\n    Enabled        bool            `json:\"enabled\"`\n    Categories     map[string]bool `json:\"categories,omitempty\"`\n    Command        string          `json:\"command,omitempty\"`         // default \"opf\"\n    TimeoutSeconds int             `json:\"timeout_seconds,omitempty\"` // default 30\n    OnFailure      string          `json:\"on_failure,omitempty\"`      // default \"warn\"\n}\n```\n\n### Design choices, justified\n\n- **Settings.json (committed), not settings.local.json.** Redaction\n  *policy* is a team decision; install state is per-machine. The `\"warn\"`\n  default protects devs who have not run `pip install opf` — they see a\n  warning, condensation proceeds with layers 1–7, push works. The existing\n  settings merge already supports per-machine override via\n  `.entire/settings.local.json`.\n- **Category strings match OPF's output labels exactly** (`private_person`,\n  not `person`). `grep private_person` shows config and OPF JSON output\n  with one query.\n- **Two-step opt-in.** Adding the block does nothing until both `enabled`\n  is true and at least one category is true. Matches the \"optional\n  add-on\" framing.\n- **`command` lets users pin to a venv** (e.g.,\n  `/Users/alice/.venvs/opf/bin/opf`) without polluting PATH.\n- **No environment variable overrides.** Consistent with the existing\n  `ConfigurePII` / `ConfigureCustomRules` precedent.\n- **Categories as `map[string]bool`, not struct fields with `*bool`.** OPF's\n  taxonomy could grow (fine-tuning to add labels is supported). A map\n  handles new categories without a `settings.go` change.\n\n### Discovery surface\n\n`entire configure --opf` interactive flow (mirrors existing `entire\nconfigure` pattern; not required for v1, removes friction for the default\ncase):\n\n1. Check if `opf` is on PATH; print install instructions if not.\n2. Walk through the eight categories with descriptions.\n3. Write the result to `.entire/settings.json`.\n\n## Output format: OPF labels → existing replacement tokens\n\nOPF emits spans like `[{\"start\": 12, \"end\": 17, \"label\": \"private_person\"}]`.\nMapping these to the existing `taggedRegion.label` field — where empty\nproduces bare `REDACTED` and non-empty produces `[REDACTED_<LABEL>]` per\n[redact.go:196-200](../../../redact/redact.go) — is one table:\n\n| OPF label | `taggedRegion.label` | Rendered token |\n|---|---|---|\n| `private_person` | `PERSON` | `[REDACTED_PERSON]` |\n| `private_email` | `EMAIL` | `[REDACTED_EMAIL]` (matches existing regex PII) |\n| `private_phone` | `PHONE` | `[REDACTED_PHONE]` (matches existing regex PII) |\n| `private_address` | `ADDRESS` | `[REDACTED_ADDRESS]` (matches existing regex PII) |\n| `private_url` | `URL` | `[REDACTED_URL]` |\n| `private_date` | `DATE` | `[REDACTED_DATE]` |\n| `account_number` | `ACCOUNT_NUMBER` | `[REDACTED_ACCOUNT_NUMBER]` |\n| `secret` | `\"\"` (empty) | `REDACTED` (bare, matches betterleaks/entropy) |\n\nLabeled tokens (rather than bare `REDACTED` for every OPF hit) keep\n\"is this over-redaction?\" feedback loops short — a human reading the\noutput can tell *why* something was redacted.\n\n**Implementation footnote:** OPF returns spans against its tokenizer's\nview of the text. The protocol adapter must verify whether the JSON\noutput uses character offsets or model-token offsets and, if the latter,\nmap them back to byte offsets at the adapter boundary. The\n[OUTPUT_SCHEMAS.md](https://github.com/openai/privacy-filter/blob/main/OUTPUT_SCHEMAS.md)\ndocument in the OPF repo is the source of truth.\n\n## Progress UX and failure messages\n\nCondensation runs inside the PostCommit hook\n([strategy/manual_commit_hooks.go](../../../cmd/entire/cli/strategy/manual_commit_hooks.go)).\nStdout/stderr from that hook is visible to the user's terminal.\n\n### Progress messages\n\n**TTY, non-accessible (default):**\n\n```\n→ OpenAI Privacy Filter: scanning transcript…\n✓ OpenAI Privacy Filter: done (1.8s)\n```\n\nBoth lines persist in scrollback — the \"done\" line is a receipt that\nthe security-sensitive opt-in feature actually ran. Silent success\nshould not look identical to silent skip.\n\n**Non-TTY (redirected, piped, CI):**\n\n```\n[entire] redaction: openai privacy filter scanning transcript (this may take a few seconds)\n[entire] redaction: openai privacy filter completed in 1.8s\n```\n\n**`ACCESSIBLE=1`:** Same as non-TTY — plain text, no spinner, no\ncarriage returns. Matches the accessibility pattern already documented\nin `CLAUDE.md`.\n\n### Failure messages (actionable, one line per cause)\n\n| Failure | Message |\n|---|---|\n| `opf` not on PATH | `× OpenAI Privacy Filter: 'opf' not found on PATH. Install with 'pip install opf' (see https://github.com/openai/privacy-filter) or set 'redaction.openai_privacy_filter.command' in .entire/settings.json. Falling back to default redaction layers.` |\n| Spawn failed (permission, etc.) | `× OpenAI Privacy Filter: failed to spawn '<command>': <err>. Falling back to default redaction layers.` |\n| Exceeded timeout | `× OpenAI Privacy Filter: exceeded <N>s timeout. Falling back to default redaction layers. Consider raising 'redaction.openai_privacy_filter.timeout_seconds' or disabling the filter.` |\n| Non-zero exit / garbled stdout | `× OpenAI Privacy Filter: <command> exited with status <N>. Falling back to default redaction layers. Last stderr: <truncated>` |\n| `on_failure: \"block\"` set | Same diagnostic + `Condensation aborted because on_failure is set to 'block'. Edit .entire/settings.json to change.` |\n\nEvery message:\n\n- Names the feature unambiguously (`OpenAI Privacy Filter`, not `OPF`) so\n  it is googleable.\n- States what failed and what the user can do.\n- States what happened to their data (`Falling back to default redaction\n  layers.`) so trust is preserved.\n- Goes to stderr; corrupting piped JSON output is unacceptable.\n\nAll messages also log through `slog.Warn` via the existing\n`componentAttr = slog.String(\"component\", \"redaction\")`\n([custom.go:41](../../../redact/custom.go)) so `entire doctor logs`\nshows them and `entire doctor bundle` captures them.\n\n## Data flow / callsite audit\n\nEvery existing `redact.JSONLBytes` / `redact.String` callsite is\nclassified as **per-turn** (stays on the plain entry point) or\n**condensation/export** (moves to the new entry point):\n\n| Callsite | Classification | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| `strategy/manual_commit_condensation.go:379` | Condensation — copies session → `entire/checkpoints/v1` | Switch to `JSONLBytesWithPrivacyFilter` |\n| `strategy/manual_commit_hooks.go:2749` | Condensation — PostCommit transcript redaction | Switch to `JSONLBytesWithPrivacyFilter` |\n| `strategy/manual_commit_hooks.go:2759` | Condensation — prompts during PostCommit | Switch to `StringWithPrivacyFilter` |\n| `checkpoint/committed.go:205, 264, 729, 1776` | Condensation — invoked transitively | Switch to `*WithPrivacyFilter` |\n| `checkpoint/committed.go:422, 840, 841, 859, 875, 1405` | Condensation — committed metadata fields | Switch to `StringWithPrivacyFilter` |\n| `checkpoint/v2_committed.go:612, 906, 1018` | Condensation (v2 path) | Switch to `*WithPrivacyFilter` |\n| `checkpoint/temporary.go:319, 390` | Per-turn temp write | **Stays on plain `JSONLBytes`** |\n| `cli/attach.go:268` | Export read — reading stored transcript back out | Switch to `JSONLBytesWithPrivacyFilter` |\n| `cli/explain.go:1111` | Export read — compacted transcript | Switch to `JSONLBytesWithPrivacyFilter` |\n| `cli/doctor_bundle.go:260, 266` | Export bundle | Switch to both `JSONLContent` and `Bytes` variants |\n| `cli/migrate.go:744, 1377, 1497, 1534` | Migration — `AlreadyRedacted` wrappers | **No change** — already-redacted bytes are not re-scanned |\n\n### Configuration bootstrap\n\n`strategy/common.go` already wires the existing redact configurators\n([common.go:373, 400](../../../cmd/entire/cli/strategy/common.go)):\n\n```go\nredact.ConfigurePII(cfg)\nredact.ConfigureCustomRules(redact.CustomRulesConfig{ ... })\n```\n\nAdd a third sibling:\n\n```go\nif s.Redaction != nil && s.Redaction.OpenAIPrivacyFilter != nil {\n    redact.ConfigurePrivacyFilter(redact.OPFConfig{\n        Enabled:        s.Redaction.OpenAIPrivacyFilter.Enabled,\n        Categories:     s.Redaction.OpenAIPrivacyFilter.Categories,\n        Command:        s.Redaction.OpenAIPrivacyFilter.Command,\n        TimeoutSeconds: s.Redaction.OpenAIPrivacyFilter.TimeoutSeconds,\n        OnFailure:      s.Redaction.OpenAIPrivacyFilter.OnFailure,\n    })\n}\n```\n\nSingle config call at startup; later calls to `*WithPrivacyFilter` read it\nthrough a guarded global the same way `getPIIConfig()` and\n`getCustomRulesConfig()` do today.\n\n## Package layout\n\nOne new file in the `redact/` package plus one stand-alone runtime\nadapter under a sub-package:\n\n```\nredact/\n├── redact.go                 (existing — regions, merge, JSONL machinery; +1 entry-point pair)\n├── pii.go                    (existing — regex PII detector)\n├── custom.go                 (existing — user rules)\n├── packs.go                  (existing — rule packs)\n├── opf.go                    (NEW — OPF detector layer + config + label mapping)\n└── opf_runtime/\n    ├── runtime.go            (NEW — interface: Redact(ctx, text, categories) → []Span)\n    ├── shellout.go           (NEW v1 — exec.Command implementation)\n    └── daemon.go             (FUTURE — only if benchmark shows daemon is worth it)\n```\n\n`redact/opf_runtime/runtime.go`:\n\n```go\ntype Runtime interface {\n    Redact(ctx context.Context, text string, categories []string) ([]Span, error)\n    Close() error\n}\n\ntype Span struct {\n    Start int\n    End   int\n    Label string  // OPF's label, e.g. \"private_person\"\n}\n```\n\n`shellout.go` spawns `opf` per call, talks JSON on stdin/stdout, respects\ncontext cancellation. ~100–150 LOC. A future `daemon.go` would implement\nthe same interface; the constructor in `opf.go` picks one based on\nsettings. **No changes to `redact/opf.go` or any callsite when the\nruntime is swapped.**\n\n## Runtime decision (deferred to benchmark)\n\nShell-out (per-call cold-start of Python + weight load) vs daemon-mode\n(load once, serve over a unix socket) is the largest remaining unknown.\nThe benchmark plan to resolve it:\n\n1. Install OPF locally: clone `openai/privacy-filter`, `pip install -e .`,\n   let weights download to `~/.opf/privacy_filter` (~1.5 GB, one-time).\n2. Pick a representative input: reuse\n   `cmd/entire/cli/transcript/compact/testdata/claude_full2.jsonl` so\n   numbers compare directly to `BenchmarkRedactJSONLBytes`.\n3. Three measurements:\n   - **Cold shell-out:** `time opf -f claude_full2.jsonl`, three fresh shells, median.\n   - **Warm in-process:** 30-line Python script that imports `opf._api`, loads model once, redacts the fixture 5 times in a loop. Time per call after the first.\n   - **Pure Python startup:** `time python -c 'import opf'` as a sanity check.\n4. Decision rule:\n   - `cold − warm` > 2s **and** frequent condensations per session → daemon pays off.\n   - `cold − warm` < 500ms → ship shell-out; daemon is busywork.\n   - In between → judgment call; lean shell-out for time-to-ship.\n\nA useful third data point: time the *entire current condensation*\nend-to-end on a real session with no OPF, to know how big the latency\nbudget is. If condensation already takes 800ms, adding 2s of OPF\ncold-start triples it; adding 200ms is noise.\n\nThe runtime choice is **decoupled from every other part of this design**.\nSettings, integration point, replacement tokens, UX, failure handling —\nnone depend on shell-out vs daemon. The decision is made before writing\nthe runtime adapter, not before approving this spec.\n\n## Testing strategy\n\nThree concentric rings mapping onto how the existing `redact` package is\ntested:\n\n### Ring 1 — Unit tests in `redact/opf_test.go` (no `opf` binary required)\n\nA fake `opf_runtime.Runtime` returns canned spans. Verifies:\n\n- `ConfigurePrivacyFilter` correctly thresholds enabled/disabled.\n- Disabled config → `detectOPF` returns nil with zero runtime calls.\n- Span-to-`taggedRegion` mapping is correct for each of the 8 OPF labels.\n- OPF regions merge correctly with regions from other layers (overlap\n  with entropy hit, overlap with betterleaks hit, label-tie-break\n  determinism).\n- Failure modes: runtime returns error → no panic, regions returned are\n  nil; `on_failure: \"block\"` propagates; `\"warn\"` does not.\n- Categories filter: if `private_person: true` but runtime returns a\n  `private_email` span anyway, that span is dropped.\n\n### Ring 2 — Adapter contract tests in `redact/opf_runtime/` (no `opf` binary required)\n\nA stub binary in `testdata/fake-opf.sh` that the shell-out runtime\ninvokes. Verifies:\n\n- Request format we send matches what `opf` accepts.\n- Response format we accept matches what `opf` documents.\n- Timeout cancellation kills the child and returns context error.\n- Non-zero exit / garbled JSON / empty stdout all become typed errors.\n- The `command` setting overrides the binary path.\n- Stderr from the child is captured into the error message.\n\n### Ring 3 — Integration tests gated by `OPF_BIN` env var (skipped in CI)\n\n`//go:build opf_integration` so they only run when explicitly invoked:\n\n```go\nfunc TestOPF_LiveBinary(t *testing.T) {\n    if os.Getenv(\"OPF_BIN\") == \"\" {\n        t.Skip(\"set OPF_BIN to run live OPF integration tests\")\n    }\n    // redact a real fixture; assert private_person spans match expected\n}\n```\n\nA handful of these catch protocol drift if OPF upstream changes its\noutput format. Devs with OPF installed run them locally; CI does not\npay the install/download cost.\n\n### Benchmark\n\nExtend `redact_bench_test.go`:\n\n- `BenchmarkRedactJSONLBytesWithPrivacyFilter` with a fake runtime —\n  measures adapter overhead, not OPF inference; stable enough to compare\n  across branches.\n- A separate `//go:build opf_integration` benchmark measures real\n  wall-clock cost.\n\n## Risks and mitigations\n\n| Risk | Mitigation |\n|---|---|\n| OPF over-redacts in practice on real transcripts | `private_person` default keeps the surface narrow; users opt up; failure path always falls back to layers 1–7; documented in error messages so users can disable cleanly. |\n| Cold-start latency makes commits feel sluggish | Latency budget known via the benchmark before implementation; daemon mode is a contained follow-up with no API churn. |\n| OPF upstream changes JSON output schema | Ring 2 stub tests pin our protocol expectations; Ring 3 live tests detect drift; failure path falls back rather than crashing. |\n| User has OPF enabled on machine A, syncs repo to machine B without `opf` installed | `on_failure: \"warn\"` default lets B proceed with layers 1–7; clear actionable error tells them what to install. |\n| Two condensations race on the same machine and both spawn `opf` | Shell-out version is naturally isolated (separate processes); future daemon needs lock-per-socket — noted in the daemon design when we get there. |\n| OPF labels span boundaries differently from regex layers | The existing `taggedRegion` merge machinery already resolves overlapping regions deterministically; labels follow first-region-wins, regions follow widest-wins. |\n\n## Open questions\n\n1. Does the OPF JSON output schema return character offsets or model-token\n   offsets? Verify against\n   [OUTPUT_SCHEMAS.md](https://github.com/openai/privacy-filter/blob/main/OUTPUT_SCHEMAS.md)\n   before writing the adapter. If tokens, the adapter does the\n   token-to-byte mapping; not visible to the rest of the package.\n2. Should `entire configure --opf` ship in the same iteration, or as a\n   follow-up? My recommendation is follow-up — the JSON schema is small\n   enough to hand-edit, and shipping the detector with a working failure\n   message is more valuable than shipping a configure helper.\n3. Whether to also surface a one-line summary in `entire status` (\"OpenAI\n   Privacy Filter: enabled (private_person)\") so users can confirm it is\n   active without opening settings. Cheap to add; deferred unless asked.\n\n## Out of scope for this iteration\n\n- Daemon mode runtime (the `daemon.go` placeholder).\n- Auto-install / venv management.\n- Fine-tuning OPF on team-specific data — supported by upstream, not\n  wired into this CLI.\n- Surfacing per-category accuracy metrics in `entire doctor`.\n- Cross-platform Windows packaging concerns for daemon mode (when we get\n  there).\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ce7696fd-e397-464a-bcd8-f722c4fa17bc","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact","sessionId":"fb93c4d8-9548-4a4c-97e6-364245c50453","version":"2.1.140","gitBranch":"openai-redact"}
