{"parentUuid":"d871c683-f57b-4e11-9217-89fab8cdd3ec","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_014wiJi8gGavAbNT1sxk8Msi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good catch — looking at OPF's own sample data tells a different story than I framed in the spec.\n\n## What OPF considers `secret` (their public examples)\n\nOnly three concrete examples exist in the public repo:\n\n| Example | Context where it appeared |\n|---|---|\n| `sk-test-XYZ987` | \"Reset credentials at … and **rotate secret** sk-test-XYZ987.\" |\n| `Priv4cy-Filt3r-2026` | \"Support ticket notes: … and **passphrase** Priv4cy-Filt3r-2026.\" |\n| `QK-TST-000-BDX5YNAL` | \"Record test_000 includes **marker** QK-TST-000-BDX5YNAL and must be archived.\" (fine-tuning demo) |\n\nAnd one `account_number`:\n- `40702810500001234567` — \"you can **send a bill for account** 40702810500001234567.\"\n\n## What this actually tells us\n\nOPF's `secret` detector is **not** \"any high-entropy string.\" It is **\"a short opaque value that surrounding human prose called a secret/passphrase/token.\"** Look at the trigger words: \"rotate secret\", \"passphrase\", \"marker\". The model is picking up on the *context*, not the shape.\n\nThat means:\n\n| Class of secret | Caught by today's stack | Caught by OPF? |\n|---|---|---|\n| OpenSSH private key blocks (`-----BEGIN…`) | ✅ betterleaks | Probably no — wrong shape |\n| AWS keys (`AKIA…`) | ✅ betterleaks | Probably no |\n| JWTs (`eyJ…`) | ✅ entropy + betterleaks | Probably no |\n| 40-char SHA hashes | ✅ entropy | No — and our docs flag this as where OPF over-fires |\n| `DB_PASSWORD=REDACTED env assignments | ✅ credential value layer | Maybe, if context is conversational |\n| **\"The new passphrase is XK-9217\"** in chat | ❌ entropy might miss (low entropy) | ✅ **OPF's sweet spot** |\n| **\"please use access code 482-991\"** | ❌ | ✅ Likely |\n| **\"my otp is 884213\"** | ❌ | ✅ Likely |\n\nThe complementarity is more useful than I claimed in the spec. OPF's `secret` plugs the hole where humans *talk about* credentials in transcripts, which is exactly the agent-transcript content this repo deals with.\n\n## Two other useful things I learned in the same look\n\n1. **`OUTPUT_SCHEMAS.md` confirms spans are character offsets**, not model-token offsets (`\"start\": 0, \"end\": 5, \"text\": \"Alice\"`). That resolves **Open Question #1** in the spec — no token-to-byte mapping needed in the adapter.\n2. **OPF's CLI emits its own placeholders** like `<PRIVATE_PERSON>` in its `redacted_text` field. We ignore that and consume the structured `detected_spans` instead, so our `[REDACTED_PERSON]` token stays consistent. Worth a one-line note in the spec.\n\n## Suggested spec updates\n\nThree small revisions, all in the same direction:\n\n1. **Section 3 footnote / Open Question #1** — resolve: character offsets, no mapping needed.\n2. **§Approach / risks table** — soften the framing that OPF's `secret` re-imports the over-redaction problem. It's still possible, but the model is more context-driven than I credited. Note that fixture-based testing during implementation is the gating step, not \"skip this category by default.\"\n3. **§Default categories** — leave `private_person` as the only default-true, **but** add a callout that `secret` is \"likely safe to enable for agent-transcript workflows and worth turning on during the benchmark phase to measure false-positive rate against `claude_full2.jsonl`.\"\n\nI'd recommend keeping the defaults conservative (still just `private_person`) and using the benchmark step to gather data on `secret` precision before recommending it as a default — but updating the spec to reflect that `secret` is a likely-good toggle, not a likely-problematic one.\n\nWant me to fold these three edits into the spec? They're all under 20 lines of changes."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":22