strings\"\n\t\"time\"\n)\n\n// shellOut runs the user-installed `opf` binary per call. Cold-start every\n// invocation is intentional for v1 — see the spec's runtime decision section.\n// Daemon mode (a future implementation of Runtime) reuses this same wire\n// format so callers do not change.\ntype shellOut struct {\n\tcommand        string\n\ttimeoutSeconds int\n}\n\ntype opfOutput struct {\n\tDetectedSpans []struct {\n\t\tLabel string `json:\"label\"`\n\t\tStart int    `json:\"start\"`\n\t\tEnd   int    `json:\"end\"`\n\t\tText  string `json:\"text\"`\n\t} `json:\"detected_spans\"`\n}\n\nfunc (s *shellOut) Redact(ctx context.Context, text string, categories []string) ([]Span, error) {\n\tif len(categories) == 0 {\n\t\treturn nil, nil // nothing to do\n\t}\n\ttimeout := time.Duration(s.timeoutSeconds) * time.Second\n\tcallCtx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(ctx, timeout)\n\tdefer cancel()\n\n\targs := []string{\n\t\t\"--device\", \"cpu\",\n\t\t\"--output-mode\", \"typed\",\n\t\t// Tell opf to read from stdin so we can stream text without a temp file.\n\t\t// Categories are passed via --labels; the wire flag name matches what\n\t\t// upstream `opf redact --help` documents.\n\t\t\"--labels\", strings.Join(categories, \",\"),\n\t}\n\n\tcmd := exec.CommandContext(callCtx, s.command, args...)\n\tcmd.Stdin = strings.NewReader(text)\n\tvar stdout, stderr bytes.Buffer\n\tcmd.Stdout = &stdout\n\tcmd.Stderr = &stderr\n\n\tif err := cmd.Run(); err != nil {\n\t\tif callCtx.Err() == context.DeadlineExceeded {\n\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"opf timeout after %s: %w\", timeout, callCtx.Err())\n\t\t}\n\t\terrMsg := strings.TrimSpace(stderr.String())\n\t\tif errMsg == \"\" {\n\t\t\terrMsg = err.Error()\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"opf exited with error: %s\", errMsg)\n\t}\n\n\tvar parsed opfOutput\n\tif err := json.Unmarshal(stdout.Bytes(), &parsed); err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"opf output not parseable as JSON: %w (stdout: %q)\", err, stdout.String())\n\t}\n\n\tout := make([]Span, 0, len(parsed.DetectedSpans))\n\tfor _, p := range parsed.DetectedSpans {\n\t\tout = append(out, Span{Start: p.Start, End: p.End, Label: p.Label})\n\t}\n\treturn out, nil\n}\n\nfunc (s *shellOut) Close() error { return nil }\n```\n\nNOTE on `--labels` flag: verify the actual flag name against `opf redact --help` during implementation. If the flag does not exist with that exact name, swap to the correct one (likely `--categories` or similar). The benchmark step in Chunk 4 also requires running `opf` locally, which is the right point to verify this against the real binary.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Remove the old stub `NewShellOut` body in `runtime.go`**\n\nIn `redact/opf_runtime/runtime.go`, remove the stubbed `shellOut` struct, `Redact`, and `Close` methods at the bottom of the file (those now live in `shellout.go`). Keep `NewShellOut` as the factory returning `&shellOut{...}` — Go's package-level resolution finds the new type.\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Run tests to verify they pass**\n\nRun: `go test ./redact/opf_runtime/ -v`\nExpected: all PASS.\n\n- [ ] **Step 7: Lint + format**\n\nRun: `mise run fmt && mise run lint`\nExpected: clean.\n\n- [ ] **Step 8: Commit Chunk 2**\n\n```bash\ngit add redact/opf_runtime/\ngit commit -m \"feat(redact): implement opf shell-out runtime adapter\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## Chunk 3: Detector + new entry points + UX\n\nThis chunk adds the eighth detection layer (`detectOPF`), the two new entry points that gate it (`StringWithPrivacyFilter` and `JSONLBytesWithPrivacyFilter`), the progress UX (TTY/non-TTY/accessible), and the failure routing (slog + stderr; warn vs block). After this chunk, the redact package can be called from anywhere with full OPF augmentation — but no production callsite has been migrated yet, so this chunk is invisible to end users.\n\n### Task 5: Implement `detectOPF` with label mapping and category filter\n\n**Files:**\n- Modify: `redact/opf.go` (add `detectOPF` and label map)\n- Test: `redact/opf_test.go`