ix is correct, but the header comment in `opf_test.go` (line 11–15) attributes the no-`t.Parallel()` decision to `t.Cleanup` races, which is accurate. No code change needed — this is a justified, beneficial deviation — but it is worth the implementer explicitly acknowledging the plan had a race bug here so the deviation is understood as deliberate, not accidental.\n\n2. **Plan's cleanup method (`ConfigurePrivacyFilter(OPFConfig{})`) replaced with `resetOPFConfig` (sets `opfConfig = nil`).** These have different semantics: `ConfigurePrivacyFilter(OPFConfig{})` installs a non-nil config with defaults applied (`Command=\"opf\"`, `Timeout=30`, `OnFailure=\"warn\"`, and a live `ShellOut` runtime), while `resetOPFConfig` sets the pointer to `nil` (true \"never configured\" state, which is what other callers check with `getOPFConfig() == nil`). The implementation's choice is strictly safer for test isolation — nil is the zero state and avoids the side effect of constructing a real `ShellOut` runtime during cleanup. No code change needed, but worth noting.\n\nMinor:\n\n1. **No test for malformed-span filtering.** The bounds guard at `/redact/opf.go:166` (`sp.Start < 0 || sp.End > len(s) || sp.Start >= sp.End`) has zero test coverage. A runtime returning a span with `Start=-1` or `Start==End` would exercise a real defensive code path. Given that Tasks 6/7 build on this function, a targeted test would reduce future regression risk. This is a gap left open by the plan itself (no such test was specified), so it is not a deviation — but adding one is cheap.\n\n2. **Slog noise during test run.** `TestDetectOPF_FailureWithWarn_ReturnsNil` emits a `WARN` line to stderr (`2026/05/13 ... WARN OpenAI Privacy Filter call failed ...`) because `handleOPFFailure` calls `slog.WarnContext` unconditionally and there is no log suppression in the test. This is cosmetically noisy but not harmful. The same pattern exists in `packs.go` tests. No action required unless the team adds a test-time de