{"parentUuid":"d2385b08-141b-40f6-9e39-8494d17be4e2","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"13e37c65-3ab6-4001-ab5c-7f3dac3ae5d6","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CwWEJpVkX7YwNLosHvBVfh","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a complete picture of the implementation. Here is the full review.\n\n---\n\n**Strengths:**\n\nThe implementation is well-structured and shows careful discipline throughout.\n\n**Spec alignment is tight.** Every callsite from the data-flow table in the spec is handled correctly. The `git grep` confirms no stray plain entry-point calls in condensation or export paths. The per-turn temporary.go isolation has an explicit explanatory comment linking to the spec — that's exactly the kind of inline documentation that survives rotation.\n\n**The entry-point pair is coherent.** All four `*WithPrivacyFilter` functions follow the exact same shape as their plain siblings: same return types, same error contracts, OPF disabled = identical to plain. The parameterization of `jsonlContentImpl` via `stringRedactor` is a clean refactor that keeps the two paths in sync structurally.\n\n**Concurrent safety is sound.** The pointer-swap pattern used by `getOPFConfig()` / `ConfigurePrivacyFilter` is the same as `getPIIConfig()` — the struct is immutable after construction, so a late `detectOPF` holding the old pointer after a reconfigure is safe. The RWMutex scope is minimal and correct.\n\n**Test coverage is comprehensive for what it covers.** The ring-1 unit tests (disabled config, label mapping, category filter, malformed span bounds) and ring-2 adapter tests (success, non-zero exit, garbled JSON, timeout, parent cancellation, category passthrough, command-not-found) are thorough and well-named. The `//nolint:ireturn` exemption for `Runtime` is properly registered in `.golangci.yaml`. The `TestLoadAndApplyRedactionSettings_WiresOPF` test closes the loop on the settings-to-runtime wire-up path.\n\n**The `redactSessionJSONLBytes` package-level var** in `manual_commit_condensation.go` is a clean test-seam that lets `condense_skip_test.go` override the injected function without touching the real path. Settings tests for OPF (load, merge, local override merge) are solid.\n\n**Resource management is correct.** Shell-out runtime spawns a fresh process per call and `Close()` is a no-op; there is nothing to leak.\n\n**Progress UX matches the spec exactly** — TTY/non-TTY/accessible branches produce the right message format, and `TestDetectOPF_EmitsProgressOnSuccess` correctly notes why it asserts \"completed in\" rather than \"done\" (the buffer is not a `*os.File` so `isTTYWriter` returns false).\n\n---\n\n**Issues:**\n\n**Critical:**\n\nNone that block correctness of the happy path.\n\n**Important:**\n\n1. `on_failure: \"block\"` is accepted by settings, stored in `OPFConfig.OnFailure`, logged by `slog.WarnContext`, and documented in the spec failure table with \"Condensation aborted because on_failure is set to 'block'.\" — but `handleOPFFailure` ignores `cfg.OnFailure` entirely and always falls through to warn behavior. The comment in `handleOPFFailure` (`In \"block\" mode, callers must propagate the returned-nil regions back up as a hard error`) describes intent that is not implemented: `handleOPFFailure` has no return value, `detectOPF` has no error return, and `StringWithPrivacyFilter`/`BytesWithPrivacyFilter` have no error return. The `JSONLBytesWithPrivacyFilter` variant does have `(RedactedBytes, error)` and could surface a block error, but the wiring does not exist. A user who sets `on_failure: \"block\"` today will see the warn behavior silently — no test exercises this path and no test exists to document that it is deferred. The plan's prose and the spec explicitly promised block enforcement. This is a shipped-setting with no enforcement. Recommendation: either add a `TODO(block-mode): not yet enforced — see issue #NNNN` comment on the `OnFailure` field and in `handleOPFFailure`, or implement the error path for the two variants that can carry it (`JSONLBytes` and `JSONLContent`). A comment-only fix prevents users from assuming the setting works.\n\n   Relevant file: `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf.go`, lines 25 and 183–199.\n\n2. `errOPFNotFound` and `opfTimeoutError` in `opf_progress.go` are typed sentinels that `formatOPFFailure` pattern-matches with `errors.Is` / `errors.As` — but `shellout.go` never produces these types. The timeout case in `shellout.go` returns `fmt.Errorf(\"opf timeout after %s: %w\", timeout, callCtx.Err())` which wraps `context.DeadlineExceeded`, not `opfTimeoutError`. The not-found case (binary missing) returns `fmt.Errorf(\"opf exited with error: %s\", errMsg)` which does not wrap `errOPFNotFound`. As a result, both the \"not found on PATH\" and \"Consider raising timeout_seconds\" branches in `formatOPFFailure` are dead code paths in production — users will always see the default fallthrough message rather than the specific actionable guidance. The unit tests for `formatOPFFailure` pass only because they inject the typed sentinels directly, hiding the mismatch.\n\n   To fix: either have `shellout.go` emit the typed sentinel errors (e.g., detect `exec.ErrNotFound` / `*os.PathError` and wrap with `errOPFNotFound`, and return `opfErrTimeout(s.timeoutSeconds)` for the deadline case), or change `formatOPFFailure` to use string-based matching against the error message. The former is cleaner and matches the original design intent.\n\n   Relevant files: `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf_runtime/shellout.go` lines 63–77 and `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf_progress.go` lines 47–78.\n\n**Minor:**\n\n3. `TestDetectOPF_DisabledReturnsNil` in `opf_test.go` includes `resetOPFConfig()` at the start of the test but not `t.Parallel()` — correct given the global. However, note that `TestDetectOPF_MapsLabelsCorrectly` uses `t.Parallel()` at both the top level and each subtest because it does not touch globals; that distinction is intentional and well-commented. No action needed, but worth confirming for anyone reading this review.\n\n4. The `TestLoad_AcceptsValidKeys` test in `settings_test.go` does not include `openai_privacy_filter` in its valid-settings JSON blob (only `pii` is present). A future stale-key validation tightening could silently drop OPF settings without a failing test to catch it. This is minor because `TestLoadFromBytes_OpenAIPrivacyFilter` provides explicit coverage, but including `openai_privacy_filter` in the valid-keys integration test would close the gap. Not a blocker.\n\n5. Commit count (7 across 4 features + 3 followup fixups) is fine for review traceability and matches the \"chunk feature + small fix\" pattern. If the PR convention for this repo is to squash before merge, the 7 commits could be condensed to 4 logical commits. This is a process preference, not a code issue.\n\n---\n\n**Assessment:** Approved-with-followups\n\nThe implementation is production-ready for the happy path and the warn failure mode. `mise run check` passes, all 4 entry points are consistent, per-turn isolation is correct, and the settings-to-runtime wire-up is fully tested. The two important issues above (`block` mode silently ignored, typed sentinel errors never produced by runtime) do not block the warn-mode use case that ships as default, but they should be addressed before users can rely on `on_failure: \"block\"` and before the \"not found on PATH\" guidance is reachable. Both fixes are contained to `opf.go` / `shellout.go` / `opf_progress.go` with no callsite impact.\n\n**Pre-PR checklist verification:**\n- `mise run check` passes: yes (all unit + integration + E2E canary pass)\n- No stray plain callsites in condensation/export: yes (grep confirms zero; only `temporary.go` and `migrate.go` use plain calls, 