ncellation, category passthrough, command-not-found) are thorough and well-named. The `//nolint:ireturn` exemption for `Runtime` is properly registered in `.golangci.yaml`. The `TestLoadAndApplyRedactionSettings_WiresOPF` test closes the loop on the settings-to-runtime wire-up path.\n\n**The `redactSessionJSONLBytes` package-level var** in `manual_commit_condensation.go` is a clean test-seam that lets `condense_skip_test.go` override the injected function without touching the real path. Settings tests for OPF (load, merge, local override merge) are solid.\n\n**Resource management is correct.** Shell-out runtime spawns a fresh process per call and `Close()` is a no-op; there is nothing to leak.\n\n**Progress UX matches the spec exactly** — TTY/non-TTY/accessible branches produce the right message format, and `TestDetectOPF_EmitsProgressOnSuccess` correctly notes why it asserts \"completed in\" rather than \"done\" (the buffer is not a `*os.File` so `isTTYWriter` returns false).\n\n---\n\n**Issues:**\n\n**Critical:**\n\nNone that block correctness of the happy path.\n\n**Important:**\n\n1. `on_failure: \"block\"` is accepted by settings, stored in `OPFConfig.OnFailure`, logged by `slog.WarnContext`, and documented in the spec failure table with \"Condensation aborted because on_failure is set to 'block'.\" — but `handleOPFFailure` ignores `cfg.OnFailure` entirely and always falls through to warn behavior. The comment in `handleOPFFailure` (`In \"block\" mode, callers must propagate the returned-nil regions back up as a hard error`) describes intent that is not implemented: `handleOPFFailure` has no return value, `detectOPF` has no error return, and `StringWithPrivacyFilter`/`BytesWithPrivacyFilter` have no error return. The `JSONLBytesWithPrivacyFilter` variant does have `(RedactedBytes, error)` and could surface a block error, but the wiring does not exist. A user who sets `on_failure: \"block\"` today will see the warn behavior silently — no test exercises this path