{"parentUuid":"c9a44abc-afda-46a3-9e40-776fd464107c","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"bf18b8d5-ae77-47ab-b568-1a553349ffea","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SrY8GdoXEoqMSpoPAVVCAX","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have everything I need. Here is the audit.\n\n---\n\n# OPF Layer — Silent-Failure Audit (PR #1214)\n\nI examined the four files you flagged, plus the settings loader (`cmd/entire/cli/settings/settings.go`) and the strategy wiring (`cmd/entire/cli/strategy/common.go`). Categorized findings below — file paths are absolute.\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL\n\n### C1. `on_failure: \"block\"` is parsed as valid but silently treated as `\"warn\"`\n**Files:**\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf.go:26-34, 210-227`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/cmd/entire/cli/settings/settings.go:906-910`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/cmd/entire/cli/strategy/common.go:416-422`\n\n**Issue:** This is the most severe silent failure in the PR. A user who adds `\"on_failure\": \"block\"` to their settings gets:\n1. No parse error — `settings.go:907` unmarshals the string into the field with no enum validation.\n2. No startup warning — `ConfigurePrivacyFilter` accepts any non-empty string at `opf.go:55-57` and stores it unchanged.\n3. No runtime warning at first invocation — `handleOPFFailure` calls `slog.WarnContext` with `on_failure=\"block\"` as an attribute (`opf.go:221`) but does not branch on the value or emit any \"block mode is unsupported, falling back to warn\" message.\n\nThe user explicitly asked for the strictest safety mode and gets the most permissive instead, with no indication anywhere that their intent was disregarded. The TODO comments are honest documentation for the developer, but the user has no way to discover them. This is exactly the silent-failure pattern that ruins trust in a privacy tool.\n\n**Recommendation:** Choose one — both have merit:\n- **Refuse to load** (safer): in the settings loader, return a `fmt.Errorf(\"on_failure=%q is not yet supported; only \\\"warn\\\" works in this build\", v)` when the value is not `\"\"` or `\"warn\"`. The user gets an immediate, actionable error on next run.\n- **Warn loudly once** (lower risk of breaking existing setups): in `ConfigurePrivacyFilter`, detect a non-`warn`/non-empty `OnFailure` and emit a `slog.WarnContext` plus a one-line stderr message via the same `opfStderr` pipe used for failure messages. Then normalize the stored value back to `\"warn\"` so logs reflect actual behavior.\n\nA bare `slog.WarnContext` is insufficient on its own — the existing OPF failure path already uses `opfStderr` precisely because users don't read `.entire/logs/entire.log`. The startup misconfiguration deserves the same treatment.\n\n### C2. Settings loader accepts any string for `on_failure` with no validation\n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/cmd/entire/cli/settings/settings.go:906-910`\n\n**Issue:** A typo like `\"on_failure\": \"bock\"` or `\"on_failure\": \"fail\"` round-trips through `json.Unmarshal` and is stored verbatim. `ConfigurePrivacyFilter` then preserves it (it only defaults the empty string at `opf.go:55-57`). Downstream, `handleOPFFailure` ignores the value entirely, so the typo silently becomes warn-mode. This is the same class of bug as C1 but with a wider blast radius: users may misspell `\"warn\"` and get the same warn behavior they intended, hiding the typo until they later try `\"block\"` and discover it also \"works.\"\n\n**Recommendation:** Treat `on_failure` as a closed enum at parse time. Add validation in the loader:\n```go\nif v, ok := raw[\"on_failure\"]; ok {\n    if err := json.Unmarshal(v, &dst.OnFailure); err != nil {\n        return fmt.Errorf(\"parsing openai_privacy_filter.on_failure: %w\", err)\n    }\n    switch dst.OnFailure {\n    case \"\", \"warn\", \"block\":\n    default:\n        return fmt.Errorf(\"openai_privacy_filter.on_failure must be \\\"warn\\\" or \\\"block\\\", got %q\", dst.OnFailure)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## IMPORTANT\n\n### I1. Spans dropped on separator-crossing have zero observability\n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf_runtime/shellout.go:163-172`\n\n**Issue:** When OPF returns a span that crosses the `\\x1e` separator, the code `continue`s with no log, no counter, no debug breadcrumb. The comment says \"rare in practice\" but the system has no way to detect when it stops being rare — e.g. if a future OPF version changes tokenizer behavior around control characters, every span could vanish and condensation would still appear successful. The user's transcript would be silently under-redacted.\n\nThis is the worst kind of silent failure in a privacy tool: data leaks become invisible.\n\n**Recommendation:** At minimum, count drops and log an aggregate at the end of `RedactBatch` when non-zero. Something like:\n```go\ndroppedAcrossSeparator := 0\ndroppedOutOfRange := 0\nfor _, p := range parsed.DetectedSpans { ... }\nif droppedAcrossSeparator > 0 || droppedOutOfRange > 0 {\n    // No slog import here yet — propagate via the returned summary or\n    // add a slog call. Privacy tooling losing detections is exactly the\n    // class of event that should be observable.\n}\n```\nThe `opf_runtime` package currently has no slog dependency. The cheapest fix is to bubble a `BatchStats` struct (or fields on an error wrapper) back to `redact.JSONLContentWithPrivacyFilter`, which already has slog and a `componentAttr`. If `len(parsed.DetectedSpans) > 0 && len(returned) == 0`, that is a near-certain sign of a wire-format break and should warn loudly.\n\n### I2. `charToByteOffset == -1` drops are silently merged with separator-crossing drops\n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf_runtime/shellout.go:164-168`\n\n**Issue:** When `charToByteOffset` returns -1, opf gave us offsets we cannot translate (charOff past the end of the rune count, or negative). This indicates one of:\n- opf returned offsets relative to a string that doesn't match `batched` (post-tokenization byte slicing, BPE token offsets, etc.)\n- opf is using a different unicode normalization\n- A wire-format change\n\nAll three are upstream bugs that the team needs to see, not opaque \"drop the span\" events. Conflating these with separator-crossing drops makes debugging hopeless: an OPF API change that breaks offset semantics looks identical to \"the user happened to have a name spanning a record-separator boundary.\"\n\n**Recommendation:** Separate the two `continue` reasons (per I1's counters), and log out-of-range drops at WARN, not DEBUG — they should never happen in a working integration.\n\n### I3. `cmd.WaitDelay = 500 * time.Millisecond` may truncate diagnostic stderr\n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf_runtime/shellout.go:133, 142-146`\n\n**Issue:** If the subprocess is killed (context cancel/timeout) and a descendant holds the pipe longer than 500ms, `cmd.Wait()` returns and `stderr.String()` is whatever was flushed before the deadline. The cmd timeout/cancel branches at lines 137-141 fire first and don't include stderr — but a non-timeout kill (e.g., a shell wrapper that intercepts SIGTERM, gets SIGKILL, then leaves a stuck child) falls through to lines 142-146, where stderr may be empty or truncated.\n\nThe user then sees `\"opf exited with error: signal: killed\"` with no context as to why. This is not a bug introduced by the WaitDelay (it would happen anyway), but the WaitDelay narrows the window where late-arriving stderr is captured.\n\n**Recommendation:** When the cmd was killed and stderr is empty, mention the WaitDelay explicitly so a user grepping for the message can find the cause:\n```go\nerrMsg := strings.TrimSpace(stderr.String())\nif errMsg == \"\" {\n    if callCtx.Err() != nil {\n        return nil, fmt.Errorf(\"opf killed by context (%v); stderr may have been truncated by %s WaitDelay: %w\",\n            callCtx.Err(), cmd.WaitDelay, err)\n    }\n    return nil, fmt.Errorf(\"opf exited with error (no stderr): %w\", err)\n}\n```\nThis is `%w`-wrapped so existing `isOPFTimeoutErr`/`isOPFNotFoundErr` matchers still work.\n\n### I4. The \"OPF ran but found nothing to scan\" path is invisible\n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/redact.go:812-846`\n\n**Issue:** Per your description (and verified at lines 832 + 842): when `inputs` is empty, `RedactBatch` is skipped entirely and the progress writer never fires. The user sees no \"scanning…\" line and no \"completed\" line. A user with OPF enabled and a transcript composed entirely of structural leaves (paths, IDs, no prose) gets bit-identical output to OPF-disabled, with no way to tell whether (a) OPF ran and found nothing or (b) OPF never ran due to misconfiguration.\n\nFor users who care enough about privacy to turn on OPF, this distinction matters. They will commit assuming OPF ran; they may not realize that a paste-heavy transcript with single-word leaf strings completely bypassed it.\n\n**Recommendation:** Add a slog.DebugContext breadcrumb when `len(inputs) == 0`:\n```go\nif len(inputs) == 0 {\n    slog.DebugContext(ctx, \"OPF: no eligible prose leaves to scan\",\n        componentAttr,\n        slog.Int(\"categories_enabled\", len(cats)),\n    )\n}\n```\nOptional but stronger: if the user has explicit OPF categories enabled and the transcript produced zero eligible inputs, emit a single-line progress note \"OpenAI Privacy Filter: no prose leaves found, skipped\" — informational, not alarming. This way the user knows the layer was considered.\n\n### I5. Span-validation drops in `detectOPF` and `JSONLContentWithPrivacyFilter` are silent\n**Files:**\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf.go:195-197`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/redact.go:856-858`\n\n**Issue:** Both sites drop spans matching `sp.Start < 0 || sp.End > len(s) || sp.Start >= sp.End`. The comment \"ignore malformed spans rather than crashing\" justifies not crashing but does not justify not logging. If OPF starts emitting malformed spans, the privacy layer silently degrades with no telemetry — same risk profile as I1/I2.\n\nYou characterized this in the prompt as \"acceptable defensive coding.\" I disagree for a privacy-critical layer. The cost of a `slog.DebugContext` with a counter is near-zero; the benefit is that \"OPF detected 47 spans but only 12 made it past validation\" is a visible signal that something is wrong.\n\n**Recommendation:** Add a single aggregated `slog.WarnContext` (or `DebugContext` if WARN is too noisy) when the validation drops more than, say, 0 spans. Counting + one log per call is sufficient — no per-span spam.\n\n---\n\n## MINOR\n\n### M1. `handleOPFFailure` always logs even when `opfStderr` write fails\n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf.go:224-226`\n\n**Issue:** `fmt.Fprintln(opfStderr, ...)` discards its error return. If `/dev/tty` was opened at startup but later closed (long-running daemon, tty hangup), the user-facing message is lost and the user only has the slog entry. Low impact because the slog entry still fires, but worth noting that the comment at `opf_progress.go:138-142` claims \"Failures still go through slog … so silent fallback never loses diagnostic info\" — which is true for the slog path but not for the stderr-write-failure path that this code doesn't handle.\n\n**Recommendation:** `fmt.Fprintln`'s error return is rarely actionable; ignoring it here is fine. But the docstring on `opfStderr` should be corrected to say \"Failures still go through slog regardless of whether the stderr write succeeds\" — small accuracy fix, not behavior.\n\n### M2. `detectOPF` short-circuits in `cfg == nil || !cfg.Enabled || cfg.runtime == nil` are intentional but `runtime == nil` after `Enabled == true` is a misconfiguration\n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf.go:169-171`\n\n**Issue:** The first three conditions are intentional skips, as you noted. But `cfg.Enabled == true && cfg.runtime == nil` should never happen in production code — `ConfigurePrivacyFilter` always constructs a runtime at line 61. It can only happen if a caller used `ConfigurePrivacyFilterWithRuntime(cfg, nil)` (which is test-only). In production, it would indicate a wiring bug — but the code silently treats it as \"OPF disabled.\"\n\n**Recommendation:** This is genuinely low priority since production paths can't hit it, but a `slog.DebugContext(ctx, \"OPF enabled but runtime is nil — misconfiguration\")` at this branch would catch any future regression where someone splits the config from runtime construction.\n\n### M3. `mapOPFLabel` collapses unknown OPF labels to bare `REDACTED` with no log\n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf.go:124-130`\n\n**Issue:** \"unknown labels collapse to bare REDACTED rather than panicking\" — fine for robustness, but if OPF adds a new label category (`\"private_id_number\"`, `\"private_iban\"`, etc.) the user has no way to discover it. They'll see `REDACTED` tokens that they could be configuring more precisely if they knew the label existed.\n\n**Recommendation:** Optional — log unknown labels at DEBUG with sampling (once per unique unknown label per process), so an attentive operator can spot OPF expansions. Skip if you consider this noise.\n\n### M4. `enabledCategories` silently filters categories not in `opfLabelMap` \n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf.go:143-145`\n\n**Issue:** If a user adds `\"categories\": { \"private_iban\": true }` (typo or future-OPF-feature), the entry silently disappears from `cats`. No warning, no startup validation. The user enabled a category and got nothing.\n\n**Recommendation:** Validate at config-load time in the settings loader — reject categories not in `opfLabelMap`, or at least slog.WarnContext at `ConfigurePrivacyFilter` time when unknown keys are present. This is the most actionable validation gap after C2.\n\n---\n\n## Things that ARE handled well\n\n(Per your instruction to acknowledge good error handling.)\n\n- The four error branches in `RedactBatch` (`shellout.go:135-147`) all return distinct, `%w`-wrapped errors. None silently swallows. The not-found / canceled / timeout / generic distinction makes downstream `isOPFNotFoundErr` / `isOPFTimeoutErr` matchers reliable.\n- `handleOPFFailure` writes to both slog AND `opfStderr` (with `/dev/tty` fallback) — dual-channel observability is the right pattern here.\n- `formatOPFFailure` always ends with \"Falling back to default redaction layers.\" — the user knows redaction continued, which is exactly the kind of explicit fallback messaging that prevents confusion.\n- The fallback in `JSONLContentWithPrivacyFilter:838-841` re-runs `jsonlContentImpl(content, String)` with the regex-only path. The user gets a redacted output and a stderr breadcrumb. Documented intent matches behavior here.\n- The category-filter `continue` (`opf.go:192-194`, `redact.go:853-855`) is correctly silent — it's defense-in-depth, not error-suppression.\n- The `runtime == nil` short-circuit in `detectOPF` correctly treats \"not configured\" as \"skip silently\" rather than crashing.\n\n---\n\n## Priority Summary\n\n1. **Fix first:** C1 (block mode TODO with no user signal) + C2 (loader accepts garbage on_failure). These are the user-visible privacy-trust bugs.\n2. **Fix soon:** I1 + I2 (separator/offset drops have no observability) — these will be the bugs that bite if OPF's wire format ever changes.\n3. **Fix when convenient:** I3 (WaitDelay stderr truncation message), I4 (no-op OPF run invisibility), I5 (validation drop counters), M4 (unknown category names).\n4. **Nice to have:** M1 (docstring fix), M2-M3 (debug logs for theoretically-impossible states).\n\nThe single most important change is C1 — `on_failure: \"block\"` going silently to warn mode is precisely the failure mode that turns this from a privacy tool into a placebo."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ac597635d5dd7630f (use SendMessage with to: 'ac597635d5dd7630f' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 75083\ntool_uses: 14\nduration_ms: 139042</usage>"}]}]},"uuid":"4fe7230a-ce77-4fef-b5c7-9ed37efd341f","timestamp":"2026-05-14T18:28:28.556Z","toolUseResult":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Look for silent failures in PR #1214 (branch `feat/openai-privacy-filter` at `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact`).\n\nFocus areas where the OPF layer might silently drop or hide errors:\n\n1. **`redact/opf_runtime/shellout.go`** — the `RedactBatch` method:\n   - When opf returns a span that crosses the batch separator, the code drops it via `continue`. Is this logged anywhere?\n   - When `charToByteOffset` returns -1 (offset out of range), the code drops via `continue`. Is this logged?\n   - When `cmd.Run()` errors, the code wraps with `%w` for not-found / canceled / timeout / generic. Are all four branches non-silent?\n   - The `cmd.WaitDelay = 500ms` recently added — if the subprocess is killed but takes longer than the WaitDelay to exit, do we lose information?\n\n2. **`redact/opf.go`** — `detectOPF` and `handleOPFFailure`:\n   - `detectOPF` short-circuits on `cfg == nil || !cfg.Enabled || cfg.runtime == nil || s == \"\"`. Each silently returns nil. Is this acceptable (yes — these are intentional skips), and is the no-prose `if !strings.ContainsRune(s, ' ') { return nil }` similarly intentional silence?\n   - When the runtime returns an error, `handleOPFFailure` is called. It logs via `slog.WarnContext` AND writes to `opfStderr`. Is the failure visible enough? Could the message be swallowed?\n   - The span-validation loop drops malformed spans (`sp.Start < 0 || sp.End > len(s) || sp.Start >= sp.End`) silently via `continue`. Is this acceptable defensive coding, or should it log?\n   - The category-filter check `if !cfg.Categories[sp.Label] { continue }` drops spans for categories the user didn't enable. Silent. Should this log? (Probably no — this is intentional filtering.)\n\n3. **`redact/redact.go`** — `JSONLContentWithPrivacyFilter`:\n   - When the batched OPF call fails (`cfg.runtime.RedactBatch` returns err), the code calls `handleOPFFailure` and falls back to `jsonlContentImpl(content, String)` — i.e., the existing 7 layers. The user gets a redacted-but-without-OPF output. Is this the documented intent? (Yes per the spec.) Is the fallback path visible to the user?\n   - The two-pass walk (collect then apply): if no leaf strings pass the has-space heuristic, `inputs` is empty and we skip the OPF call entirely. The user gets the same output as the no-OPF path with no progress message. This is good — but is it discoverable from logs that OPF \"ran but found nothing to scan\"?\n\n4. **The block-mode TODO**:\n   - `OPFConfig.OnFailure == \"block\"` is parsed and stored but NOT enforced. The TODO comments document the gap. Is this a silent failure (users set \"block\" expecting blocking, get warn behavior with no indication)? Should the settings loader warn at startup if \"block\" is configured but unsupported?\n\n5. **`charToByteOffset` edge cases** — when called with `charOff > rune count of s`, returns -1, which gets converted to a \"drop this span\" in the caller. If opf returns offsets we can't translate, those spans vanish silently. Should this log a warning since it could indicate a wire-format change in opf?\n\nReport Critical/Important/Minor with file:line refs.","agentId":"ac597635d5dd7630f","agentType":"pr-review-toolkit:silent-failure-hunter","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have everything I need. Here is the audit.\n\n---\n\n# OPF Layer — Silent-Failure Audit (PR #1214)\n\nI examined the four files you flagged, plus the settings loader (`cmd/entire/cli/settings/settings.go`) and the strategy wiring (`cmd/entire/cli/strategy/common.go`). Categorized findings below — file paths are absolute.\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL\n\n### C1. `on_failure: \"block\"` is parsed as valid but silently treated as `\"warn\"`\n**Files:**\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf.go:26-34, 210-227`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/cmd/entire/cli/settings/settings.go:906-910`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/cmd/entire/cli/strategy/common.go:416-422`\n\n**Issue:** This is the most severe silent failure in the PR. A user who adds `\"on_failure\": \"block\"` to their settings gets:\n1. No parse error — `settings.go:907` unmarshals the string into the field with no enum validation.\n2. No startup warning — `ConfigurePrivacyFilter` accepts any non-empty string at `opf.go:55-57` and stores it unchanged.\n3. No runtime warning at first invocation — `handleOPFFailure` calls `slog.WarnContext` with `on_failure=\"block\"` as an attribute (`opf.go:221`) but does not branch on the value or emit any \"block mode is unsupported, falling back to warn\" message.\n\nThe user explicitly asked for the strictest safety mode and gets the most permissive instead, with no indication anywhere that their intent was disregarded. The TODO comments are honest documentation for the developer, but the user has no way to discover them. This is exactly the silent-failure pattern that ruins trust in a privacy tool.\n\n**Recommendation:** Choose one — both have merit:\n- **Refuse to load** (safer): in the settings loader, return a `fmt.Errorf(\"on_failure=%q is not yet supported; only \\\"warn\\\" works in this build\", v)` when the value is not `\"\"` or `\"warn\"`. The user gets an immediate, actionable error on next run.\n- **Warn loudly once** (lower risk of breaking existing setups): in `ConfigurePrivacyFilter`, detect a non-`warn`/non-empty `OnFailure` and emit a `slog.WarnContext` plus a one-line stderr message via the same `opfStderr` pipe used for failure messages. Then normalize the stored value back to `\"warn\"` so logs reflect actual behavior.\n\nA bare `slog.WarnContext` is insufficient on its own — the existing OPF failure path already uses `opfStderr` precisely because users don't read `.entire/logs/entire.log`. The startup misconfiguration deserves the same treatment.\n\n### C2. Settings loader accepts any string for `on_failure` with no validation\n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/cmd/entire/cli/settings/settings.go:906-910`\n\n**Issue:** A typo like `\"on_failure\": \"bock\"` or `\"on_failure\": \"fail\"` round-trips through `json.Unmarshal` and is stored verbatim. `ConfigurePrivacyFilter` then preserves it (it only defaults the empty string at `opf.go:55-57`). Downstream, `handleOPFFailure` ignores the value entirely, so the typo silently becomes warn-mode. This is the same class of bug as C1 but with a wider blast radius: users may misspell `\"warn\"` and get the same warn behavior they intended, hiding the typo until they later try `\"block\"` and discover it also \"works.\"\n\n**Recommendation:** Treat `on_failure` as a closed enum at parse time. Add validation in the loader:\n```go\nif v, ok := raw[\"on_failure\"]; ok {\n    if err := json.Unmarshal(v, &dst.OnFailure); err != nil {\n        return fmt.Errorf(\"parsing openai_privacy_filter.on_failure: %w\", err)\n    }\n    switch dst.OnFailure {\n    case \"\", \"warn\", \"block\":\n    default:\n        return fmt.Errorf(\"openai_privacy_filter.on_failure must be \\\"warn\\\" or \\\"block\\\", got %q\", dst.OnFailure)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## IMPORTANT\n\n### I1. Spans dropped on separator-crossing have zero observability\n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf_runtime/shellout.go:163-172`\n\n**Issue:** When OPF returns a span that crosses the `\\x1e` separator, the code `continue`s with no log, no counter, no debug breadcrumb. The comment says \"rare in practice\" but the system has no way to detect when it stops being rare — e.g. if a future OPF version changes tokenizer behavior around control characters, every span could vanish and condensation would still appear successful. The user's transcript would be silently under-redacted.\n\nThis is the worst kind of silent failure in a privacy tool: data leaks become invisible.\n\n**Recommendation:** At minimum, count drops and log an aggregate at the end of `RedactBatch` when non-zero. Something like:\n```go\ndroppedAcrossSeparator := 0\ndroppedOutOfRange := 0\nfor _, p := range parsed.DetectedSpans { ... }\nif droppedAcrossSeparator > 0 || droppedOutOfRange > 0 {\n    // No slog import here yet — propagate via the returned summary or\n    // add a slog call. Privacy tooling losing detections is exactly the\n    // class of event that should be observable.\n}\n```\nThe `opf_runtime` package currently has no slog dependency. The cheapest fix is to bubble a `BatchStats` struct (or fields on an error wrapper) back to `redact.JSONLContentWithPrivacyFilter`, which already has slog and a `componentAttr`. If `len(parsed.DetectedSpans) > 0 && len(returned) == 0`, that is a near-certain sign of a wire-format break and should warn loudly.\n\n### I2. `charToByteOffset == -1` drops are silently merged with separator-crossing drops\n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf_runtime/shellout.go:164-168`\n\n**Issue:** When `charToByteOffset` returns -1, opf gave us offsets we cannot translate (charOff past the end of the rune count, or negative). This indicates one of:\n- opf returned offsets relative to a string that doesn't match `batched` (post-tokenization byte slicing, BPE token offsets, etc.)\n- opf is using a different unicode normalization\n- A wire-format change\n\nAll three are upstream bugs that the team needs to see, not opaque \"drop the span\" events. Conflating these with separator-crossing drops makes debugging hopeless: an OPF API change that breaks offset semantics looks identical to \"the user happened to have a name spanning a record-separator boundary.\"\n\n**Recommendation:** Separate the two `continue` reasons (per I1's counters), and log out-of-range drops at WARN, not DEBUG — they should never happen in a working integration.\n\n### I3. `cmd.WaitDelay = 500 * time.Millisecond` may truncate diagnostic stderr\n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf_runtime/shellout.go:133, 142-146`\n\n**Issue:** If the subprocess is killed (context cancel/timeout) and a descendant holds the pipe longer than 500ms, `cmd.Wait()` returns and `stderr.String()` is whatever was flushed before the deadline. The cmd timeout/cancel branches at lines 137-141 fire first and don't include stderr — but a non-timeout kill (e.g., a shell wrapper that intercepts SIGTERM, gets SIGKILL, then leaves a stuck child) falls through to lines 142-146, where stderr may be empty or truncated.\n\nThe user then sees `\"opf exited with error: signal: killed\"` with no context as to why. This is not a bug introduced by the WaitDelay (it would happen anyway), but the WaitDelay narrows the window where late-arriving stderr is captured.\n\n**Recommendation:** When the cmd was killed and stderr is empty, mention the WaitDelay explicitly so a user grepping for the message can find the cause:\n```go\nerrMsg := strings.TrimSpace(stderr.String())\nif errMsg == \"\" {\n    if callCtx.Err() != nil {\n        return nil, fmt.Errorf(\"opf killed by context (%v); stderr may have been truncated by %s WaitDelay: %w\",\n            callCtx.Err(), cmd.WaitDelay, err)\n    }\n    return nil, fmt.Errorf(\"opf exited with error (no stderr): %w\", err)\n}\n```\nThis is `%w`-wrapped so existing `isOPFTimeoutErr`/`isOPFNotFoundErr` matchers still work.\n\n### I4. The \"OPF ran but found nothing to scan\" path is invisible\n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/redact.go:812-846`\n\n**Issue:** Per your description (and verified at lines 832 + 842): when `inputs` is empty, `RedactBatch` is skipped entirely and the progress writer never fires. The user sees no \"scanning…\" line and no \"completed\" line. A user with OPF enabled and a transcript composed entirely of structural leaves (paths, IDs, no prose) gets bit-identical output to OPF-disabled, with no way to tell whether (a) OPF ran and found nothing or (b) OPF never ran due to misconfiguration.\n\nFor users who care enough about privacy to turn on OPF, this distinction matters. They will commit assuming OPF ran; they may not realize that a paste-heavy transcript with single-word leaf strings completely bypassed it.\n\n**Recommendation:** Add a slog.DebugContext breadcrumb when `len(inputs) == 0`:\n```go\nif len(inputs) == 0 {\n    slog.DebugContext(ctx, \"OPF: no eligible prose leaves to scan\",\n        componentAttr,\n        slog.Int(\"categories_enabled\", len(cats)),\n    )\n}\n```\nOptional but stronger: if the user has explicit OPF categories enabled and the transcript produced zero eligible inputs, emit a single-line progress note \"OpenAI Privacy Filter: no prose leaves found, skipped\" — informational, not alarming. This way the user knows the layer was considered.\n\n### I5. Span-validation drops in `detectOPF` and `JSONLContentWithPrivacyFilter` are silent\n**Files:**\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf.go:195-197`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/redact.go:856-858`\n\n**Issue:** Both sites drop spans matching `sp.Start < 0 || sp.End > len(s) || sp.Start >= sp.End`. The comment \"ignore malformed spans rather than crashing\" justifies not crashing but does not justify not logging. If OPF starts emitting malformed spans, the privacy layer silently degrades with no telemetry — same risk profile as I1/I2.\n\nYou characterized this in the prompt as \"acceptable defensive coding.\" I disagree for a privacy-critical layer. The cost of a `slog.DebugContext` with a counter is near-zero; the benefit is that \"OPF detected 47 spans but only 12 made it past validation\" is a visible signal that something is wrong.\n\n**Recommendation:** Add a single aggregated `slog.WarnContext` (or `DebugContext` if WARN is too noisy) when the validation drops more than, say, 0 spans. Counting + one log per call is sufficient — no per-span spam.\n\n---\n\n## MINOR\n\n### M1. `handleOPFFailure` always logs even when `opfStderr` write fails\n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf.go:224-226`\n\n**Issue:** `fmt.Fprintln(opfStderr, ...)` discards its error return. If `/dev/tty` was opened at startup but later closed (long-running daemon, tty hangup), the user-facing message is lost and the user only has the slog entry. Low impact because the slog entry still fires, but worth noting that the comment at `opf_progress.go:138-142` claims \"Failures still go through slog … so silent fallback never loses diagnostic info\" — which is true for the slog path but not for the stderr-write-failure path that this code doesn't handle.\n\n**Recommendation:** `fmt.Fprintln`'s error return is rarely actionable; ignoring it here is fine. But the docstring on `opfStderr` should be corrected to say \"Failures still go through slog regardless of whether the stderr write succeeds\" — small accuracy fix, not behavior.\n\n### M2. `detectOPF` short-circuits in `cfg == nil || !cfg.Enabled || cfg.runtime == nil` are intentional but `runtime == nil` after `Enabled == true` is a misconfiguration\n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf.go:169-171`\n\n**Issue:** The first three conditions are intentional skips, as you noted. But `cfg.Enabled == true && cfg.runtime == nil` should never happen in production code — `ConfigurePrivacyFilter` always constructs a runtime at line 61. It can only happen if a caller used `ConfigurePrivacyFilterWithRuntime(cfg, nil)` (which is test-only). In production, it would indicate a wiring bug — but the code silently treats it as \"OPF disabled.\"\n\n**Recommendation:** This is genuinely low priority since production paths can't hit it, but a `slog.DebugContext(ctx, \"OPF enabled but runtime is nil — misconfiguration\")` at this branch would catch any future regression where someone splits the config from runtime construction.\n\n### M3. `mapOPFLabel` collapses unknown OPF labels to bare `REDACTED` with no log\n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf.go:124-130`\n\n**Issue:** \"unknown labels collapse to bare REDACTED rather than panicking\" — fine for robustness, but if OPF adds a new label category (`\"private_id_number\"`, `\"private_iban\"`, etc.) the user has no way to discover it. They'll see `REDACTED` tokens that they could be configuring more precisely if they knew the label existed.\n\n**Recommendation:** Optional — log unknown labels at DEBUG with sampling (once per unique unknown label per process), so an attentive operator can spot OPF expansions. Skip if you consider this noise.\n\n### M4. `enabledCategories` silently filters categories not in `opfLabelMap` \n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf.go:143-145`\n\n**Issue:** If a user adds `\"categories\": { \"private_iban\": true }` (typo or future-OPF-feature), the entry silently disappears from `cats`. No warning, no startup validation. The user enabled a category and got nothing.\n\n**Recommendation:** Validate at config-load time in the settings loader — reject categories not in `opfLabelMap`, or at least slog.WarnContext at `ConfigurePrivacyFilter` time when unknown keys are present. This is the most actionable validation gap after C2.\n\n---\n\n## Things that ARE handled well\n\n(Per your instruction to acknowledge good error handling.)\n\n- The four error branches in `RedactBatch` (`shellout.go:135-147`) all return distinct, `%w`-wrapped errors. None silently swallows. The not-found / canceled / timeout / generic distinction makes downstream `isOPFNotFoundErr` / `isOPFTimeoutErr` matchers reliable.\n- `handleOPFFailure` writes to both slog AND `opfStderr` (with `/dev/tty` fallback) — dual-channel observability is the right pattern here.\n- `formatOPFFailure` always ends with \"Falling back to default redaction layers.\" — the user knows redaction continued, which is exactly the kind of explicit fallback messaging that prevents confusion.\n- The fallback in `JSONLContentWithPrivacyFilter:838-841` re-runs `jsonlContentImpl(content, String)` with the regex-only path. The user gets a redacted output and a stderr breadcrumb. Documented intent matches behavior here.\n- The category-filter `continue` (`opf.go:192-194`, `redact.go:853-855`) is correctly silent — it's defense-in-depth, not error-suppression.\n- The `runtime == nil` short-circuit in `detectOPF` correctly treats \"not configured\" as \"skip silently\" rather than crashing.\n\n---\n\n## Priority Summary\n\n1. **Fix first:** C1 (block mode TODO with no user signal) + C2 (loader accepts garbage on_failure). These are the user-visible privacy-trust bugs.\n2. **Fix soon:** I1 + I2 (separator/offset drops have no observability) — these will be the bugs that bite if OPF's wire format ever changes.\n3. **Fix when convenient:** I3 (WaitDelay stderr truncation message), I4 (no-op OPF run invisibility), I5 (validation drop counters), M4 (unknown category names).\n4. **Nice to have:** M1 (docstring fix), M2-M3 (debug logs for theoretically-impossible states).\n\nThe single most important change is C1 — `on_failure: \"block\"` going silently to warn mode is precisely the failure mode that turns this from a privacy tool into a placebo."}],"totalDurationMs":139042,"totalTokens":75083,"totalToolUseCount":14,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":739,"cache_read_input_tokens":68082,"output_tokens":6261,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":739},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":6261,"cache_read_input_tokens":68082,"cache_creation_input_tokens":739,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":739,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":8,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":6,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c9a44abc-afda-46a3-9e40-776fd464107c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact","sessionId":"fb93c4d8-9548-4a4c-97e6-364245c50453","version":"2.1.140","gitBranch":"feat/openai-privacy-filter"}
