{"parentUuid":"bceb6575-ebf2-42e1-a07c-3b31622c2399","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"52a16030-a789-47c8-a62c-0ba6a2312e42","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VRgHQxijViU9xhsb4XCVkU","type":"tool_result","content":"29\t### Optional PII redaction\n30\t\n31\tPII redaction is a separate, **opt-in** layer that runs in addition to the always-on secret detection. Disabled by default. Configured under `redaction.pii` in `.entire/settings.json` (team-shared) or `.entire/settings.local.json` (personal, gitignored).\n32\t\n33\tBuilt-in categories (when `enabled` is `true`):\n34\t\n35\t| Category | Default | Replacement token |\n36\t|---|---|---|\n37\t| `email` | on | `[REDACTED_EMAIL]` |\n38\t| `phone` | on | `[REDACTED_PHONE]` |\n39\t| `address` (US street addresses) | off (more false-positive prone) | `[REDACTED_ADDRESS]` |\n40\t\n41\tCommon bot/CI email addresses are not redacted (`noreply@*`, `actions@*`, `*@users.noreply.github.com`, `*@noreply.github.com`).\n42\t\n43\tTeams can add their own regex patterns via `custom_patterns`. Each key is a label (uppercased in the replacement token), each value is a regex string. Example: `{\"employee_id\": \"EMP-\\\\d{6}\"}` produces `[REDACTED_EMPLOYEE_ID]`.\n44\t\n45\t```json\n46\t{\n47\t  \"redaction\": {\n48\t    \"pii\": {\n49\t      \"enabled\": true,\n50\t      \"email\": true,\n51\t      \"phone\": true,\n52\t      \"address\": false,\n53\t      \"custom_patterns\": {\n54\t        \"employee_id\": \"EMP-\\\\d{6}\"\n55\t      }\n56\t    }\n57\t  }\n58\t}\n59\t```\n60\t\n61\tIf a custom pattern itself reveals sensitive structure (e.g. an internal ID format), put it in `.entire/settings.local.json` (gitignored) instead of `.entire/settings.json`.\n62\t\n63\t### Recommendations\n64\t\n65\tIf your AI sessions will touch sensitive data:\n66\t\n67\t- **Use a private repository.** This is the simplest and most complete protection. Transcripts on `entire/checkpoints/v1` are only visible to collaborators.\n68\t- **Avoid passing sensitive files to your agent.** Content that never enters the agent conversation never appears in transcripts.\n69\t- **Review before pushing.** You can inspect the `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch locally before pushing it to a remote.\n70\t\n71\t## What Gets Redacted\n72\t\n73\t### Secrets (always on)\n74\t\n75\tBetterleaks pattern matching covers cloud providers (AWS, GCP, Azure), version control platforms (GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket), payment processors (Stripe, Square), communication tools (Slack, Discord, Twilio), private key blocks (RSA, DSA, EC, PGP, OpenSSH), and generic credentials (bearer tokens, basic auth, JWTs). Dedicated credentialed URI detection covers URLs that embed passwords. Additional database connection-string detection covers DB DSNs and query-parameter passwords not reliably covered by generic secret rules. Entropy scoring catches secrets that don't match any known pattern.\n76\t\n77\tAll detected secrets are replaced with `REDACTED`. PII matches are replaced with category-tagged tokens like `[REDACTED_EMAIL]` (see [Optional PII redaction](#optional-pii-redaction)).\n78\t\n79\tTo reduce over-redaction, Entire preserves structural transcript fields such as IDs and paths, leaves placeholder values alone, and redacts only credential values for bounded key/value forms. Placeholders are detected by exact match (e.g. `changeme`, `example`, `placeholder`, `your_password`, `your_secret`, prior `REDACTED`/`[REDACTED]`/`<REDACTED>` markers) or by shape: shell expansions like `${DB_PASSWORD}`, bracketed names like `<password>` or `<your-db-password>`, and mask runs of three or more `*`/`x`/`.`/`-` (so `***`, `xxxx`, `....`, `----` all match). When a connection string contains a real password, it is redacted as a unit because partial fragments can still expose sensitive material; connection strings whose passwords are placeholders are left intact.\n80\t\n81\t## Customizing redaction\n82\t\n83\tThe built-in detectors handle well-known secret formats. For anything else you don't want stored in transcripts — internal credential shapes the bundled scanners don't know about, project codenames, or specific words and phrases you'd rather keep out of session archives — Entire offers two extension surfaces. Both run plain regex matching against transcript content, so the rules can target any string pattern, not just credentials. Both feed the same engine and run as their own layer between connection-string detection and bounded credential KV detection.\n84\t\n85\t### Surface 1: Inline `redaction.custom_redactions`\n86\t\n87\tAdd a label → regex map under `redaction.custom_redactions` in `.entire/settings.json`:\n88\t\n89\t```json\n90\t{\n91\t  \"redaction\": {\n92\t    \"custom_redactions\": {\n93\t      \"acme_token\":  \"ACME_TOKEN_[A-Za-z0-9]{20,}\",\n94\t      \"internal_id\": \"INTERNAL_[a-z]{6}_[0-9]{4}\"\n95\t    }\n96\t  }\n97\t}\n98\t```\n99\t\n100\t- The label is for diagnostics only; matches are replaced with the bare `REDACTED` token (matching the built-in secret layers, not the `[REDACTED_<LABEL>]` token used for PII).\n101\t- Regexes follow [Go's RE2 syntax](https://pkg.go.dev/regexp/syntax). No lookarounds, no backreferences.\n102\t- A failed compile is logged once at startup and the rule is skipped — it will never crash the redactor.\n103\t- Override in `.entire/settings.local.json` for personal additions; entries merge per-key (override replaces the same key, leaves other keys intact)."}]},"uuid":"7389dada-6172-4c81-a0fd-ecf2917569b8","timestamp":"2026-05-14T19:50:59.239Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/docs/security-and-privacy.md","content":"### Optional PII redaction\n\nPII redaction is a separate, **opt-in** layer that runs in addition to the always-on secret detection. Disabled by default. Configured under `redaction.pii` in `.entire/settings.json` (team-shared) or `.entire/settings.local.json` (personal, gitignored).\n\nBuilt-in categories (when `enabled` is `true`):\n\n| Category | Default | Replacement token |\n|---|---|---|\n| `email` | on | `[REDACTED_EMAIL]` |\n| `phone` | on | `[REDACTED_PHONE]` |\n| `address` (US street addresses) | off (more false-positive prone) | `[REDACTED_ADDRESS]` |\n\nCommon bot/CI email addresses are not redacted (`noreply@*`, `actions@*`, `*@users.noreply.github.com`, `*@noreply.github.com`).\n\nTeams can add their own regex patterns via `custom_patterns`. Each key is a label (uppercased in the replacement token), each value is a regex string. Example: `{\"employee_id\": \"EMP-\\\\d{6}\"}` produces `[REDACTED_EMPLOYEE_ID]`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"redaction\": {\n    \"pii\": {\n      \"enabled\": true,\n      \"email\": true,\n      \"phone\": true,\n      \"address\": false,\n      \"custom_patterns\": {\n        \"employee_id\": \"EMP-\\\\d{6}\"\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIf a custom pattern itself reveals sensitive structure (e.g. an internal ID format), put it in `.entire/settings.local.json` (gitignored) instead of `.entire/settings.json`.\n\n### Recommendations\n\nIf your AI sessions will touch sensitive data:\n\n- **Use a private repository.** This is the simplest and most complete protection. Transcripts on `entire/checkpoints/v1` are only visible to collaborators.\n- **Avoid passing sensitive files to your agent.** Content that never enters the agent conversation never appears in transcripts.\n- **Review before pushing.** You can inspect the `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch locally before pushing it to a remote.\n\n## What Gets Redacted\n\n### Secrets (always on)\n\nBetterleaks pattern matching covers cloud providers (AWS, GCP, Azure), version control platforms (GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket), payment processors (Stripe, Square), communication tools (Slack, Discord, Twilio), private key blocks (RSA, DSA, EC, PGP, OpenSSH), and generic credentials (bearer tokens, basic auth, JWTs). Dedicated credentialed URI detection covers URLs that embed passwords. Additional database connection-string detection covers DB DSNs and query-parameter passwords not reliably covered by generic secret rules. Entropy scoring catches secrets that don't match any known pattern.\n\nAll detected secrets are replaced with `REDACTED`. PII matches are replaced with category-tagged tokens like `[REDACTED_EMAIL]` (see [Optional PII redaction](#optional-pii-redaction)).\n\nTo reduce over-redaction, Entire preserves structural transcript fields such as IDs and paths, leaves placeholder values alone, and redacts only credential values for bounded key/value forms. Placeholders are detected by exact match (e.g. `changeme`, `example`, `placeholder`, `your_password`, `your_secret`, prior `REDACTED`/`[REDACTED]`/`<REDACTED>` markers) or by shape: shell expansions like `${DB_PASSWORD}`, bracketed names like `<password>` or `<your-db-password>`, and mask runs of three or more `*`/`x`/`.`/`-` (so `***`, `xxxx`, `....`, `----` all match). When a connection string contains a real password, it is redacted as a unit because partial fragments can still expose sensitive material; connection strings whose passwords are placeholders are left intact.\n\n## Customizing redaction\n\nThe built-in detectors handle well-known secret formats. For anything else you don't want stored in transcripts — internal credential shapes the bundled scanners don't know about, project codenames, or specific words and phrases you'd rather keep out of session archives — Entire offers two extension surfaces. Both run plain regex matching against transcript content, so the rules can target any string pattern, not just credentials. Both feed the same engine and run as their own layer between connection-string detection and bounded credential KV detection.\n\n### Surface 1: Inline `redaction.custom_redactions`\n\nAdd a label → regex map under `redaction.custom_redactions` in `.entire/settings.json`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"redaction\": {\n    \"custom_redactions\": {\n      \"acme_token\":  \"ACME_TOKEN_[A-Za-z0-9]{20,}\",\n      \"internal_id\": \"INTERNAL_[a-z]{6}_[0-9]{4}\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- The label is for diagnostics only; matches are replaced with the bare `REDACTED` token (matching the built-in secret layers, not the `[REDACTED_<LABEL>]` token used for PII).\n- Regexes follow [Go's RE2 syntax](https://pkg.go.dev/regexp/syntax). No lookarounds, no backreferences.\n- A failed compile is logged once at startup and the rule is skipped — it will never crash the redactor.\n- Override in `.entire/settings.local.json` for personal additions; entries merge per-key (override replaces the same key, leaves other keys intact).","numLines":75,"startLine":29,"totalLines":231}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bceb6575-ebf2-42e1-a07c-3b31622c2399","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact","sessionId":"fb93c4d8-9548-4a4c-97e6-364245c50453","version":"2.1.140","gitBranch":"feat/openai-privacy-filter","slug":"tranquil-sprouting-shore"}
